===============================
UNIT TESTING THEORY (JUnit - Kotlin)
===============================

1) WHAT IS UNIT TESTING?
-------------------------
Unit Testing is a software testing technique where we test
a small piece of code (usually a single function or class)
in isolation to verify that it behaves as expected.

In our case:
We tested -> applyDiscount() function
Class under test -> DiscountCalculator
Test class -> DiscountCalculatorTest

Goal:
✔ Verify correct calculations
✔ Verify edge cases
✔ Verify exceptions
✔ Prevent future bugs (regression safety)

------------------------------------------------------------

2) AAA PATTERN (Used in Our Test)
----------------------------------

Every good unit test follows:

A → Arrange   (Prepare data)
A → Act       (Call the method)
A → Assert    (Verify result)

Example:

// Arrange
val price = 100.0
val discount = 10

// Act
val result = calculator.applyDiscount(price, discount)

// Assert
assertEquals(90.0, result, 0.001)

------------------------------------------------------------

3) WHAT IS ASSERTION?
----------------------

An Assertion is a statement that checks whether something is true.

If assertion condition is TRUE → Test PASSES
If assertion condition is FALSE → Test FAILS

Assertions are provided by JUnit framework.

------------------------------------------------------------

4) IMPORTANT ASSERT METHODS (TABLE FORMAT)
--------------------------------------------

| Method                         | Purpose                                      | When To Use |
|--------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|-------------|
| assertEquals(expected, actual, delta) | Checks if expected == actual          | Comparing numbers (Double, Float) |
| assertEquals(expected, actual) | Checks equality (no delta)                   | String, Int, Boolean |
| assertNotEquals(expected, actual) | Ensures values are NOT equal             | Negative testing |
| assertTrue(condition)          | Passes if condition is TRUE                  | Boolean checks |
| assertFalse(condition)         | Passes if condition is FALSE                 | Boolean checks |
| assertNull(value)              | Passes if value is NULL                      | Nullable testing |
| assertNotNull(value)           | Passes if value is NOT NULL                  | Object existence |
| assertSame(expected, actual)   | Checks same memory reference                 | Object reference testing |
| assertNotSame(expected, actual)| Checks different references                  | Object identity testing |
| assertThrows(Exception::class.java) | Verifies exception is thrown          | Error handling tests |
| fail(message)                  | Forces test to fail manually                 | Unexpected execution |

------------------------------------------------------------

5) EXPLANATION OF EACH ASSERT (THEORY)
----------------------------------------

1️⃣ assertEquals()

Definition:
Checks whether expected value equals actual value.

Important for floating numbers:
We use delta because floating-point numbers
have small precision differences.

Example:
assertEquals(90.0, result, 0.001)

Without delta:
Double comparisons may fail even if visually equal.

------------------------------------------------------------

2️⃣ assertNotEquals()

Checks that two values are NOT equal.

Useful when:
We want to ensure incorrect result is not returned.

------------------------------------------------------------

3️⃣ assertTrue()

Passes only if condition evaluates to TRUE.

Example:
assertTrue(result == 90.0)

Usually used for:
• Boolean results
• Logical conditions

------------------------------------------------------------

4️⃣ assertFalse()

Passes only if condition is FALSE.

Example:
assertFalse(result == 100.0)

------------------------------------------------------------

5️⃣ assertNull()

Passes if object is NULL.

Used for:
• Nullable variables
• Optional return values

------------------------------------------------------------

6️⃣ assertNotNull()

Passes if object is NOT NULL.

Used to confirm:
• Object was created
• Method did not return null

------------------------------------------------------------

7️⃣ assertSame()

Checks if two references point to SAME object in memory.

Example:
val obj1 = "Hello"
val obj2 = obj1

assertSame(obj1, obj2)  // PASS

------------------------------------------------------------

8️⃣ assertNotSame()

Checks if references are DIFFERENT objects.

Even if values are equal, memory reference may differ.

------------------------------------------------------------

9️⃣ assertThrows()

Definition:
Checks whether a specific exception is thrown.

Example:
assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException::class.java) {
    calculator.applyDiscount(-100.0, 10)
}

If exception NOT thrown → Test FAILS
If correct exception thrown → Test PASSES

Used for:
• Validation rules
• Error conditions
• Input constraints

------------------------------------------------------------

🔟 fail()

Manually fails the test.

Example:
fail("This code should not be reached")

Used when:
Execution reaches unexpected code.

